## Native object files on the command line (the section 12 rows of
## 2026-10-26 'M22.c extended (Rust-found defect 3)' and 'M22.e extended
## (Rust-found defects 1-4, tests)'; ImplementationPlan.md 5.9.4, 5.10.8):
## rustc passes an empty native object (symbols.o) under
## -Clinker-plugin-lto, and msil-ld stopped with 'unknown file type'.
## - A native ELF or COFF object, in the object format of the triple's OS,
##   that defines no symbol, or only symbols nothing references, is skipped
##   (at most a note, as for unreferenced native archive members): no error
##   or warning, and the output is the one without it, for CIL objects and
##   for bitcode (LTO);
## - a referenced definition in native object code stays the error
##   'symbol '<s>' is defined only in native object code (<file>); MSIL
##   cannot link machine code', also when a native library or a .NET library
##   msil-ld wrote defines the same symbol, before or after the object (M22
##   review r2 finding 1): an import library (nat.lib) and a -shared library
##   (m/natlib.dll) on the Windows triple, a shared object (libnat.so) on
##   linux-gnu; --trace-symbol shows no library definition, so nothing binds
##   the symbol elsewhere;
## - an oldnames-style alias object on the command line (strdup.obj, weak
##   external strdup -> _strdup with IMAGE_WEAK_EXTERN_SEARCH_ALIAS) binds
##   through its alias as the same object does inside oldnames.lib
##   (native-coff-alias.test): strdup is the P/Invoke
##   pinvokeimpl("api-ms-win-crt-string-l1-1-0.dll" as "_strdup") (review r2
##   finding 6);
## - a file of a format msil-ld does not link names the format it found (a
##   WebAssembly object).
## coff-code.yaml (Inputs/native) defines fn1, fn2 and dataobj (-D FN1=
## renames fn1).

# RUN: rm -rf %t && split-file %s %t && cd %t
